10.1.5. Static Classes & Methods # In some conditions you may want to create static classes that provide static methods without
instantiation. That means you do not need to use the new operator keyword to create a variable
of the class type. Also, without the class instance variable, you access the members of a static
class by using the class name itself. For example, if you have a static class called SomeMath: static class SomeMath
{
   public static int Sum(int a, int b)    // a public static method
   {
      return a + b;
   }
} To use the Sum method in the SomeMath class, you call it directly without creating a new object: internal class

10.1.3. Using a Class # In the following example, there is a class called Program with a Main method. We define another class
called DoMath and then, in the Main method of the Program class, 1) instantiate an object of the DoMath class by
using the new keyword and 2) use the dot notation ( . ) to access a method defined in DoMath: internal class Program
{
   static void Main(string[] args)
   {
      DoMath doMath = new DoMath();    // create an object using the new keyword
      int aSum = doMath.Sum(2, 2);     // use the [object].[method] syntax to use the methods
      Console.WriteLine(aSum);         // output 4
   }
}

class DoMath

4.2.5. Step in loop header # i++ is a common pattern and it increments by 1. Also common in the iterator/step section of
the loop header is using assignment statement to control stepping: 1 int i = 10 ; 2 int k = 10 ; 3 i = i + k ; or the equivalent short-hand compound assignment operator: 1 int i = 10 ; 2 int k = 10 ; 3 i += k ; This means to increment the variable i by k. Most C# binary operations have a similar variation. For instance
if op is + , - , * , / or % , variable op = expression means the same as variable = variable op expression For example x *= 5 ; is the same as x = x * 5 ;

